  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\n***Wednesday/Thursday***\n\nA longwave ridge looks to still be overhead at 500-mb Wednesday\nbased on GEFS and EC ensemble guidance. At the same time, a \nshortwave trough centered over California looks to begin moving \nanticyclonically around the ridge. A surface low at the leading \nedge of the trough is forecast to provide some showers and \nthunderstorms in the area Wednesday afternoon and evening. The \nbest chances for precipitation are across portions of Eastern \nColorado, where NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance shows a 50-70% \nchance for greater than 0.1 inches of rain. Chances that these \nthunderstorms become severe appear marginal at this time, as \nLREF guidance and GFS/EC model soundings show 0-6 km shear \nmaxing out around 30 to 35 kts. The greatest threat with these \nstorms would be brief wind gusts around 60 mph, as GFS and \nNAMNST soundings show the possibility of an inverted-V sounding \nprofile, favorable for microbursts. As the shortwave trough \nmoves east of the forecast area, chances for showers on Thursday\nreduce dramatically. NBM precipitation guidance shows most of \nthe forecast area at less than a one in three chance to receive \ngreater than 0.1 inches of rain. Confidence in severe weather \noccurring is highest on Wednesday, though is still only around \n5-10%. High temperatures both Wednesday and Thursday are \nforecast in the 90s across the area.\n\n***Friday/Saturday***\n\nAs the trough begins to move southeastward across the Midwest \nFriday, a cold front may begin breaking down the upper level \nridge to the east of the forecast area. This will cause the \nridge axis to translate westward into the Four-Corners Region, \nand establish approximately north-northwesterly flow over the \narea at 500-mb. High temperatures could hover in the lower 90s \nFriday and Saturday as this pattern favors northeasterly surface\nflow. Precipitation chances continue to fall during this \nperiod, as PoPs (probability of precipitation) fall below 15% \nacross nearly the entire area.\n\n***Sunday/Monday***\n\nEnsemble guidance supports another strong trough moving across \nthe Northern United States and Southern Canada Sunday. This \nwould once again bring the ridge axis over the forecast region, \nbut with a positively tilted orientation. Temperatures may rise \ninto the mid and upper 90s once again Sunday and Monday as this \noccurs. Drier conditions are forecast as well, as GEFS 850-mb \nheight mean-spread guidance supports approximately southwesterly\nsurface flow under this pattern. Relative humidity (RH) values \nmay drop as low as the lower 20s on Sunday, and upper teens on \nMonday. While these RHs are low, they do not meet critical fire \nweather criteria (<15%). Additionally, NBM guidance suggests \nthat wind gusts have less than a 50% chance of exceeding 30 mph \nacross most of the area either day. The main exceptions are \nportions of Northeastern Colorado and Southwest Nebraska, where \nthere is up to a 70% chance. As such, the development of \ncritical fire weather conditions across this zone may be \nsomething to keep an eye on, especially since fuels are drier \nacross these areas.",
